            "heading": {
              "type": "text",
              "name": "Heading",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<h2>The chai was never the point</h2>",
                "type_preset": "h2"
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "body": {
              "type": "text",
              "name": "Body",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>After graduating I spent several months in India, and the way people drink chai there was impossible to miss. Not sit-down cafés — roadside stalls, gallons of it on the boil all day, for anyone walking past.</p><p>The chaiwalas were holding something together. They knew their regulars by name. None of it was transactional.</p><p>American coffee culture doesn't ask anything of the person serving you, or of the person being served. I think that's part of why this country is as lonely as it is. So the chai isn't the end goal — it's the reason to stand still for a minute and talk to someone.</p>",
                "type_preset": "rte",
                "max_width": "normal"
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
