    "why_ka": {
      "type": "why-ka",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "Why it's called Ka",
        "body": "<p>India's languages aren't dialects of one another — many come from entirely different families. Naming the company after any one of them would have meant leaving everyone else out. Calling it “Indian” or “desi” felt like saying nothing at all.</p><p>But all of them teach the alphabet the same way. The vowels come first, then the consonants — <em>ka, kha, ga, gha</em> — and every script starts that list with the same sound.</p><p>So the company is named after the one letter they share.</p>",
        "glyph_alt": "The letter ka, written in thirteen Indian scripts",
        "caption": "“Ka”, in thirteen Indian scripts. The same sound, and almost nothing in common.",
        "closing": "An Indian company, built for America. All of it.",
        "color_scheme": "scheme-2",
        "section_width": "page-width",
        "padding-block-start": 80,
